(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["amritabindu"]={"slug":"amritabindu","name":"Amṛtabindu","deva":"अमृतबिन्दु","note":"","intro":["A minor Upaniṣad (22 verses) of the <strong>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</strong> recension, classed among the twenty <strong>Yoga Upaniṣads</strong> and the five <strong>Bindu Upaniṣads</strong> (Amṛtabindu, Amṛtanāda, Nādabindu, Dhyānabindu, Brahmabindu).","Sanskrit text source: sanskritdocuments.org (`amrtabindu_upan.itx`, encoded by Anshuman Pandey, proofread by M. Giridhar &amp; Sunder Hattangadi). Cross-checked against T.R.S. Ayyangar (Adyar Library, 1938) and Paul Deussen, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (1897)."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Amṛta-bindu Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<strong>अमृतबिन्दु उपनिषत्</strong> — <em>\"The Drop of Immortality\"</em>","A minor Upaniṣad (22 verses) of the <strong>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</strong> recension, classed among the twenty <strong>Yoga Upaniṣads</strong> and the five <strong>Bindu Upaniṣads</strong> (Amṛtabindu, Amṛtanāda, Nādabindu, Dhyānabindu, Brahmabindu).","Sanskrit text source: sanskritdocuments.org (`amrtabindu_upan.itx`, encoded by Anshuman Pandey, proofread by M. Giridhar &amp; Sunder Hattangadi). Cross-checked against T.R.S. Ayyangar (Adyar Library, 1938) and Paul Deussen, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (1897)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Manuscript variations at a glance","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Edition</td><td>Verses</td><td>Veda</td><td>Notes</td></tr><tr><td>T.R.S. Ayyangar (Adyar, 1938)</td><td>22</td><td>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</td><td>The most widely cited recension. Treats the <em>prāṇa</em> section as a separate text (Amṛtanāda Up.). Used as the basis here.</td></tr><tr><td>Sanskritdocuments.org</td><td>22</td><td>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</td><td>Same recension as Ayyangar.</td></tr><tr><td>Swami Madhavananda (Advaita Ashrama)</td><td>22</td><td>Atharvaveda (as titled)</td><td>Same 22-verse text; veda-affiliation differs by region.</td></tr><tr><td>Paul Deussen (1897)</td><td>38</td><td>Atharvaveda</td><td>Conflates Amṛtabindu with Amṛtanāda; first 22 ≈ our text.</td></tr><tr><td>Brahma-Yogin recension</td><td>22</td><td>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</td><td>Basis of Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin's Sanskrit <em>bhāṣya</em>.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five commentaries summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</strong> (early 18th c., Kāñcī) — the <em>only</em> surviving classical Sanskrit <em>bhāṣya</em> on the minor Upaniṣads (108 in total). Pure Advaita Vedānta in the Śaṅkara line. Stresses <em>jīvan-mukti</em>, dismantles every dualism the verses raise, and reads the closing \"I am Vāsudeva\" as a Vedāntic <em>mahā-vākya</em>-equivalent.","2. <strong>Swami Sivananda</strong> (Divine Life Society, <em>Twenty Principal Upanishads</em>; <em>Yoga Vedanta Forest Academy</em> notes) — practical <em>sādhanā</em>-oriented Advaita. Frames each verse as a meditation instruction; gives detailed treatment of \"churning the mind\" (v. 20) as <em>manana-mathana</em>.","3. <strong>Swami Krishnananda</strong> (Divine Life Society lectures, <em>The Mandukya Upanishad</em> corpus &amp; associated minor-Upaniṣad notes) — philosophical-existential reading. Emphasises <em>avasthā-traya</em> analysis (v. 11) and the pot-space simile (v. 13) as a refutation of jīva-paricchinnatva.","4. <strong>T.R.S. Ayyangar</strong> (<em>The Yoga Upaniṣads</em>, Adyar Library, 1938) — the standard English translation; conservatively literal, follows Brahma-Yogin closely, with footnotes on textual variants and the Amṛtanāda overlap.","5. <strong>Paul Deussen</strong> (<em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em>, 1897; Eng. trans. Bedekar/Palsule, Motilal 1980) — Indological reading. Reconstructs an original 38-verse text, treats vv. 1–10 as a \"vidyā of the mind\", vv. 11–14 as an <em>avasthā</em>-passage parallel to Māṇḍūkya, and the closing verses as a later Bhāgavata interpolation (note the \"Vāsudeva\" of v. 22).","<em>On usage below</em>: under each verse the <strong>Brahma-Yogin</strong> line is the classical Advaitin lens, <strong>Sivananda</strong> is meditative-practical, <strong>Krishnananda</strong> is philosophical-Advaita, <strong>Ayyangar</strong> captures the literal/translator's note, <strong>Deussen</strong> captures the Indological/textual angle."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha (Peace invocation)","title":"","sa":["ॐ सह नाववतु । सह नौ भुनक्तु । सह वीर्यं करवावहै ।","तेजस्विनावधीतमस्तु । मा विद्विषावहै ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥ हरिः ॐ ॥"],"iast":["Oṃ saha nāv-avatu | saha nau bhunaktu | saha vīryaṃ karavāvahai | tejasvi nāv adhītam astu | mā vidviṣāvahai || Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ || hariḥ Oṃ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"May He protect us both (teacher and pupil); may He nourish us both; may we both work together with vigour; may our study be illumined; may we not hate. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace. Hariḥ Oṃ.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(Standard Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda <em>śānti-mantra</em>.)"]}]},{"t":"The twenty-two verses","u":[{"ref":"The twenty-two verses","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each verse below is given as: <strong>Devanāgarī → IAST → pada-by-pada gloss → literal translation → paraphrase → 5-commentary digest.</strong>","<em>Grammar abbreviations:</em> m./f./n. = masc./fem./neut. gender; nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc. = case; sg./du./pl. = number; pres./impf./pf./pot./impv./ger./inf./caus. = tense/mood/non-finite."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verse 1","title":"Two minds, pure and impure","sa":["मनो हि द्विविधं प्रोक्तं शुद्धं चाशुद्धमेव च ।","अशुद्धं कामसंकल्पं शुद्धं कामविवर्जितम् ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["mano hi dvividhaṃ proktaṃ śuddhaṃ cāśuddham eva ca |","aśuddhaṃ kāma-saṃkalpaṃ śuddhaṃ kāma-vivarjitam || 1 ||"],"words":[["manaḥ","n. nom. sg.","the mind"],["hi","indecl. (emphatic)","indeed"],["dvi-vidham","n. nom. sg. adj.","two-fold"],["proktam","n. nom. sg. pf.pass.part. of pra-vac","(is) declared"],["śuddham","n. nom. sg. adj.","pure"],["ca","conj.","and"],["aśuddham","n. nom. sg. adj.","impure"],["eva","indecl. (emphatic)","only/just"],["ca","conj.","and"],["aśuddham","n. nom. sg. adj.","the impure (one)"],["kāma-saṃkalpam","n. nom. sg. tatpuruṣa","desire-resolving / charged with volitions of desire"],["śuddham","n. nom. sg.","the pure (one)"],["kāma-vivarjitam","n. nom. sg. karmadhāraya","desire-abandoned / free of desire"]],"lit":"\"Mind, indeed, is declared two-fold — pure and impure only; the impure is (that which is) the volition of desire, the pure (is that which is) free of desire.\"","para":"Mind has two modes. When it generates <em>saṃkalpa</em> (resolve, intention) towards objects of desire it is impure; emptied of <em>kāma</em>, the very same mind is pure.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the distinction is not ontological but functional — there is one <em>manas</em> whose two states are <em>malina</em> (with <em>vāsanā</em>) and <em>nirmala</em> (cleared of <em>vāsanā</em>). The whole text is then a programme for moving from the first to the second."],["Sivananda","\"Cease willing and the mind becomes the seat of Self.\" Translates <em>kāma-saṃkalpa</em> practically as the chain of <em>rāga-dveṣa</em> impulses that arise pre-thought."],["Krishnananda","reads <em>saṃkalpa</em> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka sense (1.5.3) — the mind <em>is</em> its <em>saṃkalpas</em>; purity is the cessation of self-othering intention, not the suppression of thought-content."],["Ayyangar","renders \"Manas is declared to be of two kinds, pure and impure; that which is associated with the thought of desire is impure, that which is devoid of desire is pure.\""],["Deussen","the verse opens his \"Reinheit-des-Geistes\" section (vv. 1–4); he notes the Yoga-Vāsiṣṭha (III.112) parallel on <em>śuddha/aśuddha cittam</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 2","title":"Mind is the cause of bondage and liberation","sa":["मन एव मनुष्याणां कारणं बन्धमोक्षयोः ।","बन्धाय विषयासक्तं मुक्त्यै निर्विषयं स्मृतम् ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["mana eva manuṣyāṇāṃ kāraṇaṃ bandha-mokṣayoḥ |","bandhāya viṣayāsaktaṃ muktyai nirviṣayaṃ smṛtam || 2 ||"],"words":[["manaḥ","n. nom. sg.","the mind"],["eva","indecl.","alone"],["manuṣyāṇām","m. gen. pl.","of human beings"],["kāraṇam","n. nom. sg.","(is the) cause"],["bandha-mokṣayoḥ","m. gen. du.","of bondage-and-liberation"],["bandhāya","m. dat. sg.","for bondage"],["viṣaya-āsaktam","n. nom. sg. (kc.)","attached-to-objects"],["muktyai","f. dat. sg.","for liberation"],["nir-viṣayam","n. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","object-less"],["smṛtam","n. nom. sg. pf.pass.part. of smṛ","is remembered / is traditionally taught"]],"lit":"\"The mind alone is the cause, for human beings, of both bondage and liberation; (mind) attached to objects is (the cause) of bondage, (mind) free of objects is taught for liberation.\"","para":"The single instrument of <em>saṃsāra</em> and of <em>mokṣa</em> is one and the same mind. Direction, not nature, decides the outcome.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>kāraṇatā</em> here is <em>upādāna-like</em> — mind is the material out of which both predicaments are formed. Cites parallels in Maitrāyaṇī Up. 6.34 (\"<em>cittam eva hi saṃsāraḥ</em>\")."],["Sivananda","the most-quoted verse of the text in modern <em>yoga</em> literature; he uses it as the thesis of his <em>Mind: Its Mysteries and Control</em>."],["Krishnananda","the agency is not the mind <em>as substance</em> but the mind <em>as relation</em> — <em>viṣaya-āsakti</em> is itself the bondage, not a precursor to it."],["Ayyangar","\"Manas verily is, for mankind, the cause of bondage and of liberation...\" — flags <em>smṛtam</em> as marking received tradition rather than fresh insight."],["Deussen","parallels Bhāgavad-Gītā 6.5–6 (<em>uddhared ātmanātmānam</em>) and Maitri Up. 6.34; he treats this as the <em>Leitsatz</em> of the whole text."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 3","title":"Object-free mind is the goal","sa":["यतो निर्विषयस्यास्य मनसो मुक्तिरिष्यते ।","अतो निर्विषयं नित्यं मनः कार्यं मुमुक्षुणा ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["yato nirviṣayasyāsya manaso muktir iṣyate |","ato nirviṣayaṃ nityaṃ manaḥ kāryaṃ mumukṣuṇā || 3 ||"],"words":[["yataḥ","indecl. (correl.)","since / from which"],["nir-viṣayasya","n. gen. sg.","of (the) object-less"],["asya","pron. n. gen. sg.","of this"],["manasaḥ","n. gen. sg.","of mind"],["muktiḥ","f. nom. sg.","liberation"],["iṣyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of iṣ","is desired / is held"],["ataḥ","indecl.","therefore"],["nir-viṣayam","n. acc. sg.","(made) object-less"],["nityam","adv.","constantly"],["manaḥ","n. acc. sg.","the mind"],["kāryam","n. acc. sg. gerundive","(is) to-be-made"],["mumukṣuṇā","m. instr. sg.","by the one-seeking-liberation"]],"lit":"\"Since liberation is held (to belong) to this object-less mind, therefore the mind is constantly to be made object-less by the seeker of liberation.\"","para":"If <em>mokṣa</em> belongs only to the <em>nirviṣaya</em> mind, the <em>mumukṣu</em>'s standing duty is to empty mind of objects — not occasionally, but <em>nityam</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","gerundive <em>kāryam</em> turns the previous metaphysical claim into a <em>vidhi</em> (injunction). The \"object-less mind\" is provisional language for <em>manonāśa</em>."],["Sivananda","connects directly to <em>pratyāhāra</em>; the daily <em>abhyāsa</em> is the practical content of <em>nityam</em>."],["Krishnananda","warns against reading <em>nirviṣaya</em> as \"no thoughts\" — it is the absence of the <em>subject-object split</em>, not of mental events."],["Ayyangar","notes the verse as the <em>vidhi-vākya</em> that grounds the rest of the work as a sādhanā-text rather than a <em>jñāna</em>-only Upaniṣad."],["Deussen","aligns with Yogasūtra 1.2 (<em>citta-vṛtti-nirodhaḥ</em>) and reads vv. 1–3 as the Upaniṣadic prototype of Patañjali's opening."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 4","title":"Withdrawn mind reaches the supreme state","sa":["निरस्तविषयासङ्गं संनिरुद्धं मनो हृदि ।","यदाऽऽयात्यात्मनो भावं तदा तत्परमं पदम् ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["nirasta-viṣayāsaṅgaṃ saṃniruddhaṃ mano hṛdi |","yadāyāty ātmano bhāvaṃ tadā tat paramaṃ padam || 4 ||"],"words":[["nirasta-viṣaya-āsaṅgam","n. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","(one) whose attachment-to-objects has been cast off"],["saṃ-niruddham","n. nom. sg. pp.","well-restrained"],["manaḥ","n. nom. sg.","mind"],["hṛdi","n. loc. sg. of hṛd","in the heart"],["yadā","indecl. correl.","when"],["ā-yāti","3 sg. pres. of ā-yā","enters / attains"],["ātmanaḥ","m. gen. sg.","of the Self"],["bhāvam","m. acc. sg.","the being/state"],["tadā","indecl.","then"],["tat","pron. n. nom. sg.","that"],["paramam","n. nom. sg. adj.","supreme"],["padam","n. nom. sg.","station/abode"]],"lit":"\"When the mind — its attachment to objects cast off, well-restrained in the heart — enters the being of the Self, then (it is) that supreme station.\"","para":"Restrain the un-attached mind in the <em>hṛdaya</em>; when it enters the <em>ātman</em>-mode, that itself is the highest <em>pada</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>hṛd</em> is not the anatomical heart but the <em>daharākāśa</em> of Chāndogya 8.1; <em>bhāvam āyāti</em> = <em>tādātmyam āpadyate</em>."],["Sivananda","descriptive of the experience of <em>sahaja samādhi</em>; he stages it after <em>trāṭaka</em> and <em>japa</em> drills."],["Krishnananda","the construction <em>ātmano bhāvam</em> is striking — the <em>ātman</em> has no <em>bhāva</em> but its own; entering it is not arriving somewhere but ceasing to be elsewhere."],["Ayyangar","\"When manas, free from attachment to objects, is well-restrained in the heart and reaches the state of ātman, then is that supreme state attained.\""],["Deussen","the <em>paramam padam</em> is the same as Kaṭha 3.9; he marks v. 4 as climax of section 1."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 5","title":"Restrain until the heart-clinging is destroyed","sa":["तावदेव निरोद्धव्यं यावद्धृदि गतं क्षयम् ।","एतज्ज्ञानं च ध्यानं च शेषो न्यायश्च विस्तरः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["tāvad eva niroddhavyaṃ yāvad dhṛdi gataṃ kṣayam |","etaj jñānaṃ ca dhyānaṃ ca śeṣo nyāyaś ca vistaraḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["tāvat","adv. correl.","so long"],["eva","indecl.","only"],["nir-oddhavyam","n. nom. sg. gerundive","to-be-restrained"],["yāvat","adv. correl.","as long as"],["hṛdi","loc. sg.","in the heart"],["gatam","n. nom. sg. pp.","gone (i.e. the going-into / the vāsanā-track)"],["kṣayam","m. acc. sg.","(to) destruction"],["etat","pron. n. nom. sg.","this"],["jñānam","n. nom. sg.","knowledge"],["ca","conj.","and"],["dhyānam","n. nom. sg.","meditation"],["ca","conj.","and"],["śeṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the rest"],["nyāyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(mere) reasoning"],["ca","conj.","and"],["vistaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","(verbal) elaboration"]],"lit":"\"Only so long is (the mind) to be restrained as the going-(of-it)-in-the-heart has gone to destruction; this is knowledge and meditation; the rest is (mere) logic and verbal expansion.\"","para":"Restraint is needed only until <em>vāsanā</em>-traffic in the heart ceases. That cessation <em>is</em> both <em>jñāna</em> and <em>dhyāna</em>; everything else is talk.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","a strong anti-scholastic note — once the mind dissolves, neither <em>nyāya</em> nor <em>vistara</em> is needed; the verse anticipates v. 18 (discard the granthas)."],["Sivananda","\"Sādhanā is a means, not the goal; the means is to be dropped when the goal is reached.\" Used as a corrective to mechanical <em>abhyāsa</em>."],["Krishnananda","<em>hṛdi gatam</em> is the <em>liṅga-śarīra</em>'s root-grip; <em>kṣayam</em> is its dissolution, not a stilling of one thought-stream."],["Ayyangar","renders <em>nyāya</em> as \"polemical reasoning\" — distinguishes the text's <em>jñāna</em> from the technical Nyāya <em>darśana</em>."],["Deussen","sees here the seam between an early <em>yoga</em> core (vv. 1–5) and the Vedāntic exposition (vv. 6 ff.) — section break in his German edition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 6","title":"Beyond thinkable and unthinkable","sa":["नैव चिन्त्यं न चाचिन्त्यं न चिन्त्यं चिन्त्यमेव च ।","पक्षपातविनिर्मुक्तं ब्रह्म सम्पद्यते तदा ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["naiva cintyaṃ na cācintyaṃ na cintyaṃ cintyam eva ca |","pakṣapāta-vinirmuktaṃ brahma sampadyate tadā || 6 ||"],"words":[["na eva","indecl.","not at all"],["cintyam","n. nom. sg. gerundive","to-be-thought / thinkable"],["na ca","indecl.","nor"],["a-cintyam","n. nom. sg.","un-thinkable"],["na cintyam","repeat","not thinkable"],["cintyam eva ca","repeat (+emphatic)","yet (also) just thinkable"],["pakṣa-pāta-vinirmuktam","n. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","free from siding-with-(either)-position"],["brahma","n. acc. sg.","(to) Brahman"],["sam-padyate","3 sg. pres. mid. of sam-pad","one-attains / one-becomes"],["tadā","indecl.","then"]],"lit":"\"Neither thinkable, nor un-thinkable, nor (only) thinkable, and yet just thinkable — free from siding-with-either-position one then attains Brahman.\"","para":"Drop the categories <em>cintya / acintya</em> themselves. When the mind no longer takes sides, it becomes Brahman.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","a <em>catuṣkoṭi</em>-style negation echoing Māṇḍūkya 7's <em>avyavahāryam, agrāhyam</em>. Brahman is reached not by any thought-mode but by the cessation of taking-sides."],["Sivananda","practical advice — at the height of <em>dhyāna</em> drop both \"I am thinking\" and \"I am not thinking\"; the residual <em>pakṣa</em> is the last <em>vikalpa</em>."],["Krishnananda","notes the structural parallel to Nāgārjuna's <em>catuṣkoṭi</em> but reads it Advaitically — Brahman is <em>not</em> śūnya, it is the <em>substratum</em> of all four alternatives."],["Ayyangar","\"Then is Brahman attained, free from one-sided views\" — emphasises <em>pakṣapāta</em> as adhyāropa-clinging."],["Deussen","reads the verse as a deliberate echo of Kena 1.3 (<em>anyad eva tad viditāt atho aviditād adhi</em>)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 7","title":"Through the syllable, beyond the syllable","sa":["स्वरेण संदधेद्योगमस्वरं भावयेत्परम् ।","अस्वरेणानुभावेन नाभावो भाव इष्यते ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["svareṇa sandhayed yogam asvaraṃ bhāvayet param |","asvareṇānubhāvena nābhāvo bhāva iṣyate || 7 ||"],"words":[["svareṇa","m. instr. sg.","by the sound/syllable (i.e. Oṃ)"],["sandhayet","3 sg. pot. caus. of sam-dhā","one should join"],["yogam","m. acc. sg.","(the) yoga (union)"],["a-svaram","n. acc. sg.","the soundless"],["bhāvayet","3 sg. pot. caus. of bhū","one should contemplate"],["param","n. acc. sg.","the supreme"],["a-svareṇa","n. instr. sg.","by the soundless"],["anu-bhāvena","m. instr. sg.","by direct experience"],["na abhāvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","not non-being"],["bhāvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","being"],["iṣyate","3 sg. pres. pass.","is regarded"]],"lit":"\"With the sound one should join the yoga; the soundless, the supreme, one should contemplate. By the soundless realisation it is held (to be) being, not non-being.\"","para":"Use <em>Oṃ</em> (the audible <em>svara</em>) to enter yoga; then contemplate the <em>asvara</em> (the silent, partless Brahman). The result of that silent realisation is not nothingness but pure <em>being</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","exactly the Māṇḍūkya path — <em>Oṃ</em> as <em>upāya</em>, the <em>amātra</em> fourth as <em>upeya</em>. <em>Na abhāvaḥ</em> refutes śūnyavāda."],["Sivananda","maps this onto <em>japa → ajapā → laya</em> — audible to mental to silent stages of repetition."],["Krishnananda","the verse pivots from method (<em>Oṃ</em>) to <em>ontology</em> (<em>bhāva</em>); the soundless is not absence-of-sound but the fullness on which sound depends."],["Ayyangar","\"By the soundless realisation, it is regarded as bhāva, not as abhāva\" — flags the polemic against Buddhist <em>śūnyatā</em>."],["Deussen","his §3, \"Vom Oṃ\"; compares Praśna 5 on <em>Oṃ-upāsanā</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 8","title":"That partless Brahman: 'I am That'","sa":["तदेव निष्कलं ब्रह्म निर्विकल्पं निरञ्जनम् ।","तद्ब्रह्माहमिति ज्ञात्वा ब्रह्म सम्पद्यते ध्रुवम् ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["tad eva niṣkalaṃ brahma nirvikalpaṃ nirañjanam |","tad brahmāham iti jñātvā brahma sampadyate dhruvam || 8 ||"],"words":[["tat","pron. n. nom. sg.","that"],["eva","indecl.","itself"],["niṣ-kalam","n. nom. sg. adj.","partless"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman"],["nir-vikalpam","n. nom. sg.","free of constructions"],["nir-añjanam","n. nom. sg.","unstained"],["tat brahma aham iti","quote-formula","\"that Brahman (am) I\""],["jñātvā","ger. of jñā","having known"],["brahma","n. acc. sg.","Brahman"],["sampadyate","3 sg. pres. mid.","one attains"],["dhruvam","adv.","certainly"]],"lit":"\"That very (Brahman is) partless, free of constructions, unstained Brahman; having known 'I am that Brahman', one certainly attains Brahman.\"","para":"The <em>asvara</em> of v. 7 is <em>niṣkala, nirvikalpa, nirañjana</em>. The recognition \"I am That\" is sufficient — Brahman is <em>certainly</em> attained.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>jñātvā sampadyate</em> is the <em>aikya</em>-recognition; not a process but an immediate identification. Cites <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em> (Bṛhad. 1.4.10)."],["Sivananda","triple negation (<em>niṣkala-nirvikalpa-nirañjana</em>) is the standard Advaita lakṣaṇa; he uses this verse as a meditation seed."],["Krishnananda","the <em>dhruvam</em> is significant — the result of right <em>jñāna</em> is <em>necessary</em>, not probabilistic."],["Ayyangar","explicitly groups this with the <em>mahā-vākyas</em>."],["Deussen","notes parallels to Śvetāśvatara 6.19 (<em>niṣkala, niṣkriya</em>)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 9","title":"Beyond evidence, beyond beginning","sa":["निर्विकल्पमनन्तं च हेतुदृष्टान्तवर्जितम् ।","अप्रमेयमनादिं च यज्ज्ञात्वा मुच्यते बुधः ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["nirvikalpam anantaṃ ca hetu-dṛṣṭānta-varjitam |","aprameyam anādiṃ ca yaj jñātvā mucyate budhaḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["nir-vikalpam","n. acc. sg.","undifferentiated"],["an-antam","n. acc. sg.","endless"],["ca","conj.","and"],["hetu-dṛṣṭānta-varjitam","n. acc. sg. (kc.)","free of (logical) ground-and-example"],["a-prameyam","n. acc. sg.","not knowable-by-pramāṇa"],["an-ādim","n. acc. sg.","beginningless"],["ca","conj.","and"],["yat","rel. pron. n. acc. sg.","which"],["jñātvā","ger.","having known"],["mucyate","3 sg. pres. pass.","is liberated"],["budhaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the wise (one)"]],"lit":"\"(That) undifferentiated, endless, free of (logical) reason-and-example, immeasurable, beginningless — having known which, the wise one is liberated.\"","para":"Brahman cannot be reached by <em>hetu</em> (logical reason) or <em>dṛṣṭānta</em> (analogy). The wise are freed by knowing this <em>unreasoned</em> reality.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","explicit anti-Nyāya stance — <em>anumāna</em> cannot reach Brahman; only <em>śruti</em> + <em>anubhava</em>."],["Sivananda","warns the seeker against demanding \"proofs\"; the realisation is <em>self-luminous</em>."],["Krishnananda","expands <em>aprameya</em> — Brahman is not an <em>object</em> of any <em>pramāṇa</em> including perception, inference, or scripture in the objective mode; only <em>sākṣātkāra</em>."],["Ayyangar","careful: <em>hetu-dṛṣṭānta-varjitam</em> renders as \"devoid of (the requirements of) probans and example\"."],["Deussen","compares Kaṭha 6.12 (\"<em>naiva vācā na manasā prāptum śakyaḥ</em>\")."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 10","title":"Neither bound nor freed","sa":["न निरोधो न चोत्पत्तिर्न बद्धो न च साधकः ।","न मुमुक्षुर्न वै मुक्त इत्येषा परमार्थता ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["na nirodho na cotpattir na baddho na ca sādhakaḥ |","na mumukṣur na vai mukta ity eṣā paramārthatā || 10 ||"],"words":[["na","indecl.","not"],["ni-rodhaḥ","m. nom. sg.","restraint / suppression"],["na ca","indecl.","nor"],["ut-pattiḥ","f. nom. sg.","origination"],["na baddhaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(anyone) bound"],["na ca sādhakaḥ","m. nom. sg.","nor (any) practiser"],["na mu-mukṣuḥ","m. nom. sg.","nor (any) liberation-seeker"],["na vai muktaḥ","m. nom. sg.","nor indeed (any) liberated-one"],["iti","indecl.","thus"],["eṣā","f. nom. sg. pron.","this"],["paramārthatā","f. nom. sg.","the supreme truth"]],"lit":"\"No restraint, no origination, no one bound, no practitioner, no liberation-seeker, no liberated one indeed — this is the supreme truth.\"","para":"Ultimately, the whole vocabulary of bondage and release — and even the seeker — is provisional. <em>Paramārtha-satya</em> is that there is nothing to be done.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","this is the <em>paramārtha</em> register, identical to Gauḍapāda's Kārikā 2.32 (<em>na nirodho na cotpattiḥ...</em>); the verse is a near-verbatim source for, or echo of, that famous <em>kārikā</em>."],["Sivananda","warns this is the <em>jñānin's</em> statement, not the <em>abhyāsin's</em> — the seeker still has to practise, but should know the final view."],["Krishnananda","the verse uproots <em>adhyāsa</em> at every level; the very <em>path</em> is provisional language."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the Gauḍapāda parallel and treats this verse as the <em>siddhānta</em> of the work."],["Deussen","the cornerstone of his <em>ajāti-vāda</em> reading; he reproduces it in his <em>Allgemeine Geschichte der Philosophie</em> as a Vedāntic locus classicus."],["Textual note","Some manuscripts read <em>muktaḥ</em> (nom.) for <em>mukta</em>; metre and sense identical."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 11","title":"One Self in waking, dream, deep sleep","sa":["एक एवात्मा मन्तव्यो जाग्रत्स्वप्नसुषुप्तिषु ।","स्थानत्रयव्यतीतस्य पुनर्जन्म न विद्यते ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["eka evātmā mantavyo jāgrat-svapna-suṣuptiṣu |","sthāna-traya-vyatītasya punarjanma na vidyate || 11 ||"],"words":[["eka","adj. nom. sg.","one"],["eva","indecl.","only"],["ātmā","m. nom. sg.","(the) Self"],["mantavyaḥ","m. nom. sg. gerundive of man","(is) to-be-thought-(of)"],["jāgrat-svapna-suṣuptiṣu","f. loc. pl. (dvandva)","in waking-dream-deep-sleep"],["sthāna-traya-vyatītasya","m. gen. sg. (bahuvr.)","of (the one) gone beyond the three states"],["punar-janma","n. nom. sg.","re-birth"],["na vidyate","3 sg. pres. pass.","does not exist"]],"lit":"\"The Self is to be regarded as only one in waking, dream and deep sleep; for the one who has gone beyond the three states, re-birth does not exist.\"","para":"Across the three <em>avasthās</em>, one and the same Self abides. Transcending those three states ends <em>punar-janma</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the entire <em>avasthā-traya-viveka</em> of Māṇḍūkya 3–7 is compressed here. <em>Sthāna-traya-vyatīta</em> = <em>turīya</em>."],["Sivananda","a key meditation point — track the \"I\" through three states; the constant is <em>ātman</em>."],["Krishnananda","the verse undoes the <em>ahaṅkāra</em>'s identification with one state; <em>turīya</em> is not a fourth state but the <em>substratum</em> of the other three."],["Ayyangar","\"(He) for whom the three states have ceased — for him there is no re-birth.\""],["Deussen","matches Māṇḍūkya 7; he treats this as direct evidence of the closeness of the two texts."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 12","title":"One Self perceived as many: the moon in water","sa":["एक एव हि भूतात्मा भूते भूते व्यवस्थितः ।","एकधा बहुधा चैव दृश्यते जलचन्द्रवत् ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["eka eva hi bhūtātmā bhūte bhūte vyavasthitaḥ |","ekadhā bahudhā caiva dṛśyate jalacandravat || 12 ||"],"words":[["eka","adj.","one"],["eva hi","emphatic","only indeed"],["bhūta-ātmā","m. nom. sg.","(the) Self-of-beings"],["bhūte bhūte","n. loc. sg. distrib.","in each being"],["vy-ava-sthitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","abiding"],["ekadhā","adv.","in one way / as one"],["bahudhā","adv.","in many ways"],["ca eva","conj. + emphatic","and (also)"],["dṛśyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of dṛś","is seen"],["jala-candra-vat","adv.","like (the) water-moon"]],"lit":"\"The Self-of-beings is verily one only, abiding in every being; (it) is seen as one and as many, like the moon in water.\"","para":"One <em>bhūtātman</em> lives in each being. It <em>appears</em> one or many in the way the moon (one) appears in many pools (many).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>jala-candra</em> simile is canonical for <em>pratibimba-vāda</em> — Brahman is one, reflected variously in <em>upādhis</em>."],["Sivananda","useful preacher's verse — the same Self in all; ethical corollary is <em>sarvabhūta-hitam</em>."],["Krishnananda","careful — the simile shows <em>appearance</em> of multiplicity, not <em>real</em> multiplicity. The <em>jīva</em>'s \"many-ness\" is an <em>upādhi</em>-phenomenon."],["Ayyangar","\"...is seen as one as well as many, like the moon in (different) waters.\""],["Deussen","parallels Kaṭha 5.9–11 (<em>agniḥ ekaḥ ... rūpaṃ rūpam pratirūpo babhūva</em>)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 13","title":"The pot-space simile","sa":["घटसंवृतमाकाशं नीयमानो घटे यथा ।","घटो नीयेत नाकाशः तद्वज्जीवो नभोपमः ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["ghaṭa-saṃvṛtam ākāśaṃ nīyamāno ghaṭe yathā |","ghaṭo nīyeta nākāśaḥ tadvaj jīvo nabhopamaḥ || 13 ||"],"words":[["ghaṭa-saṃvṛtam","n. nom. sg. (kc.)","pot-enclosed"],["ākāśam","n. nom. sg.","space/ether"],["nīyamānaḥ","m. nom. sg. pres.pass.part.","being carried"],["ghaṭe","m. loc. sg.","in the pot (case-attraction; sense: \"as the pot is moved\")"],["yathā","conj.","as"],["ghaṭaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the pot"],["nīyeta","3 sg. pot. pass.","would be carried"],["na ākāśaḥ","m. nom. sg.","not the space"],["tadvat","adv.","likewise"],["jīvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the individual self"],["nabhaḥ-upamaḥ","m. nom. sg. bahuvr.","sky-like"]],"lit":"\"As the space enclosed by a pot — when the pot is being moved, the pot would be carried, not the space — so the jīva is sky-like.\"","para":"When you move a pot you do not move the space within it. Likewise the <em>jīva</em>'s seeming travel through <em>saṃsāra</em> is a feature of its <em>upādhi</em>, not of the <em>ātman</em>-space.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>ghaṭākāśa</em> simile is foundational to Advaita; he traces it to Yogavāsiṣṭha and the Pañcadaśī tradition."],["Sivananda","\"The body moves, the Self does not move.\" Used to dissolve identification with bodily location."],["Krishnananda","the simile <em>negates the very container-content distinction</em> at the limit — pot-space and great-space are not two spaces but appear two because of the pot."],["Ayyangar","notes the textual difficulty of <em>nīyamāno ghaṭe</em> (loc. for the moving pot) — translates \"when the pot is moved\"."],["Deussen","compares Maitri 6.30 (the pot-space metaphor expanded over several verses)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 14","title":"Pots break, space remains","sa":["घटवद्विविधाकारं भिद्यमानं पुनः पुनः ।","तद्भिन्नं/तद्भग्नं न च जानाति स जानाति च नित्यशः ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["ghaṭavad vividhākāraṃ bhidyamānaṃ punaḥ punaḥ |","tad-bhinnaṃ na ca jānāti sa jānāti ca nityaśaḥ || 14 ||"],"words":[["ghaṭa-vat","adv.","pot-like"],["vividha-ākāram","n. acc. sg. (kc.)","of-various-forms"],["bhidya-mānam","n. acc. sg. pres.pass.part.","being broken"],["punaḥ punaḥ","adv.","again and again"],["tat-bhinnam","n. acc. sg. (kc.)","that-broken / when-that-(pot)-is-broken"],["na ca jānāti","3 sg. pres. neg.","(the space) does not know (the breaking)"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg. pron.","it (the space/Self)"],["jānāti ca","3 sg. pres. + conj.","yet knows"],["nityaśaḥ","adv.","always"]],"lit":"\"Pot-like, of various shapes, being broken again and again — when that (pot) is broken, (the space) does not know it; yet it itself knows always.\"","para":"Bodies (like pots) are many-shaped and keep breaking. The Self neither registers the breaking as its own, nor ever ceases to know.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the apparent paradox (<em>na jānāti / jānāti</em>) is resolved as: the Self does not know <em>the upādhi-breaking as an affecting event</em>, yet is itself <em>cit</em>-knowing always."],["Sivananda","the verse undercuts the fear of death — body-pots break; the <em>ātman</em>-sky is untouched."],["Krishnananda","the <em>nityaśaḥ</em> knowing is <em>svayam-prakāśa</em>, not propositional knowing; it is the <em>light</em> by which any \"knowing of\" occurs."],["Ayyangar","prefers <em>tad-bhinnam</em> (the reading our edition uses); flags <em>tad-bhagnam</em> as a known variant."],["Deussen","reads the verse as a transition between pot-simile and <em>māyā</em>-passages (vv. 15 ff.)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 15","title":"Word-māyā veils, but on the broken veil, one is seen as one","sa":["शब्दमायावृतो नैव तमसा याति पुष्करे ।","भिन्ने तमसि चैकत्वमेक एवानुपश्यति ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["śabda-māyāvṛto naiva tamasā yāti puṣkare |","bhinne tamasi caikatvam eka evānupaśyati || 15 ||"],"words":[["śabda-māyā-āvṛtaḥ","m. nom. sg. (kc.)","covered by word-illusion"],["na eva","indecl.","not at all"],["tamasā","n. instr. sg.","by darkness"],["yāti","3 sg. pres.","goes / reaches"],["puṣkare","n. loc. sg. of puṣkara","in the lotus (here = paramaṃ padam)"],["bhinne","n. loc. sg. abs.","when (it is) broken"],["tamasi","n. loc. sg. abs.","the darkness"],["ca","conj.","and"],["ekatvam","n. acc. sg.","oneness"],["ekaḥ eva","m. nom. sg. + emph.","one alone"],["anu-paśyati","3 sg. pres. of anu-dṛś","sees / contemplates"]],"lit":"\"(One) covered by word-illusion, by darkness, does not reach the (supreme) lotus; when the darkness is broken, oneness alone is seen as one.\"","para":"Words are themselves <em>māyā</em>; veiled in that darkness one cannot reach the <em>puṣkara</em> (Brahman). When the veil parts, only oneness is seen, and as one.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>śabda-māyā</em> = the <em>adhyāropa</em> by language; <em>puṣkara</em> is glossed via Chāndogya 8.1.5 (<em>daharaṃ puṇḍarīkam</em>)."],["Sivananda","polemical against <em>śābdika</em> learning untransformed into <em>anubhava</em>."],["Krishnananda","language structures perception; <em>māyā</em> here is the conceptual scheme itself, not a cosmic veil."],["Ayyangar","renders <em>puṣkare</em> as \"the heart-lotus\" — the inner <em>daharākāśa</em>."],["Deussen","compares Bṛhad. 4.4.22 (<em>śabda</em> as the way most are misled)."],["Textual note","This verse is the most variant-prone in the text; some MSS read <em>na yāti</em> for <em>naiva...yāti</em> and <em>puṣkaram</em> (acc.) for <em>puṣkare</em> (loc.). Our reading follows sanskritdocuments.org / Ayyangar."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 16","title":"The sound-imperishable and the silent-imperishable","sa":["शब्दाक्षरं परं ब्रह्म तस्मिन्क्षीणे यदक्षरम् ।","तद्विद्वानक्षरं ध्यायेद्यदीच्छेच्छान्तिमात्मनः ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["śabdākṣaraṃ paraṃ brahma tasmin kṣīṇe yad akṣaram |","tad-vidvān akṣaraṃ dhyāyed yadīcchec chāntim ātmanaḥ || 16 ||"],"words":[["śabda-akṣaram","n. nom. sg.","sound-imperishable (Oṃ as syllable)"],["param brahma","n. nom. sg.","the supreme Brahman"],["tasmin","pron. n. loc. sg.","when that"],["kṣīṇe","n. loc. sg. abs.","(is) exhausted/dissolved"],["yat akṣaram","rel. + n. nom. sg.","(the other) imperishable that (remains)"],["tat","n. acc. sg.","that"],["vidvān","m. nom. sg. pres.part. of vid","the knower"],["akṣaram","n. acc. sg.","(that) imperishable"],["dhyāyet","3 sg. pot. of dhyai","should meditate"],["yadi icchet","3 sg. pot. of iṣ","if (one) would wish"],["śāntim","f. acc. sg.","peace"],["ātmanaḥ","m. gen. sg.","of the Self"]],"lit":"\"(Oṃ is) the sound-imperishable, the supreme Brahman; when that is dissolved, the (other) imperishable that remains — that imperishable the knower should meditate on, if he wishes peace of the Self.\"","para":"<em>Oṃ</em> is the <em>śabda-akṣara</em> — a supreme but uttered Brahman. When that fades, the <em>amātra</em> (silent) imperishable remains. Meditate on <em>that</em> for the Self's peace.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","exactly the four-mātrā / <em>amātra</em> analysis of Māṇḍūkya 8–12. <em>Śabda-akṣara</em> = <em>Oṃ</em> with mātrās; the <em>kṣīṇa-residue</em> = <em>amātra-turīya</em>."],["Sivananda","the <em>ajapā-japa</em> tradition's locus classicus."],["Krishnananda","even <em>Oṃ</em> is provisional; the <em>true</em> imperishable is what remains after the syllable itself subsides."],["Ayyangar","\"Oṃ which is the imperishable Word, is the higher Brahman...\""],["Deussen","his \"Lautloses Brahman\" section header; parallels Mahā-Nārāyaṇa 24.1."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 17","title":"Two knowledges, śabda-brahma and parā","sa":["द्वे विद्ये वेदितव्ये तु शब्दब्रह्म परं च यत् ।","शब्दब्रह्मणि निष्णातः परं ब्रह्माधिगच्छति ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["dve vidye veditavye tu śabda-brahma paraṃ ca yat |","śabda-brahmaṇi niṣṇātaḥ paraṃ brahmādhigacchati || 17 ||"],"words":[["dve","adj. f. nom. du.","two"],["vidye","f. nom. du.","knowledges"],["veditavye","f. nom. du. gerundive","to-be-known"],["tu","particle","indeed"],["śabda-brahma","n. nom. sg.","the word-Brahman"],["param","n. nom. sg.","the supreme"],["ca yat","conj. + rel.","and what (is)"],["śabda-brahmaṇi","n. loc. sg.","in the word-Brahman"],["niṣṇātaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","one who has bathed-in / is proficient"],["param brahma","n. acc. sg.","(to) supreme Brahman"],["adhi-gacchati","3 sg. pres. of adhi-gam","attains"]],"lit":"\"Two knowledges indeed are to be known: the word-Brahman and what is supreme; (the one who is) proficient in the word-Brahman attains the supreme Brahman.\"","para":"There are two <em>vidyās</em>: <em>śabda-brahma</em> (the Vedic/sound knowledge) and <em>para-brahma</em>. Mastery of the first leads to the second.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","an explicit <em>parā-/aparā-vidyā</em> pairing (Muṇḍaka 1.1.4–5), and a defence of the value of textual study as a means."],["Sivananda","refutes both rejections — neither study alone nor dismissal of study; <em>niṣṇāta</em> (deeply versed) is the precondition for transcendence."],["Krishnananda","aligns with Muṇḍaka but notes the verse's <em>graded</em> model — text <em>leads to</em> the para, not as cause but as ladder."],["Ayyangar","literal: \"Two are the <em>vidyās</em> to be known\"; flags the Muṇḍaka parallel in his footnote."],["Deussen","his <em>Schriftwissen und Selbstwissen</em> section; canonical <em>para/apara</em> pairing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 18","title":"Discard the books as one discards the husk","sa":["ग्रन्थमभ्यस्य मेधावी ज्ञानविज्ञानतत्परः ।","पलालमिव धान्यार्थी त्यजेद्ग्रन्थमशेषतः ॥ १८ ॥"],"iast":["grantham abhyasya medhāvī jñāna-vijñāna-tatparaḥ |","palālam iva dhānyārthī tyajed grantham aśeṣataḥ || 18 ||"],"words":[["grantham","m. acc. sg.","the text/book"],["abhyasya","ger. of abhi-as","having studied"],["medhāvī","m. nom. sg.","the intelligent (one)"],["jñāna-vijñāna-tatparaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","intent-on knowledge-and-direct-realisation"],["palālam","m./n. acc. sg.","husk / straw"],["iva","indecl.","as"],["dhānya-arthī","m. nom. sg.","one wanting grain"],["tyajet","3 sg. pot. of tyaj","should abandon"],["grantham","acc. sg.","the text"],["a-śeṣa-taḥ","adv.","entirely"]],"lit":"\"Having studied the text, the intelligent one — intent on knowledge and realisation — should entirely abandon the text, as one who wants grain (abandons) the husk.\"","para":"Texts are <em>upāya</em>. Study them, then drop them entirely, the way a winnower drops the straw to keep the grain.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>abhyasya</em> (<em>after</em> mastery, not before) is critical — the verse is not anti-study; it is anti-clinging."],["Sivananda","the most famous verse of the text in modern <em>vyākhyāna</em> literature; he ties it directly to v. 5 (<em>śeṣaḥ nyāyaś ca vistaraḥ</em>)."],["Krishnananda","draws an epistemological line — texts are <em>signs</em>, not <em>Brahman</em>; clinging to the signs blocks the signified."],["Ayyangar","renders <em>jñāna-vijñāna</em> as \"knowledge and (direct) experience\"."],["Deussen","compares the famous Bṛhad. 4.4.21 (<em>tam eva dhīro vijñāya prajñāṃ kurvīta brāhmaṇaḥ</em>)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 19","title":"Milk from many-coloured cows is one colour","sa":["गवामनेकवर्णानां क्षीरस्याप्येकवर्णता ।","क्षीरवत्पश्यते ज्ञानं लिङ्गिनस्तु गवां यथा ॥ १९ ॥"],"iast":["gavām aneka-varṇānāṃ kṣīrasyāpy ekavarṇatā |","kṣīravat paśyate jñānaṃ liṅginas tu gavāṃ yathā || 19 ||"],"words":[["gavām","f. gen. pl.","of cows"],["aneka-varṇānām","f. gen. pl. (kc.)","of many colours"],["kṣīrasya","n. gen. sg.","of the milk"],["api","indecl.","also/even"],["eka-varṇatā","f. nom. sg. abstr.","one-colour-ness"],["kṣīra-vat","adv.","milk-like"],["paśyate","3 sg. pres. mid. (kvip.)","one sees"],["jñānam","n. acc. sg.","(real) knowledge"],["liṅginaḥ","m. acc. pl.","(the) bearers-of-marks / (variously-marked) bodies"],["tu","particle","but"],["gavām","f. gen. pl.","of cows"],["yathā","conj.","as"]],"lit":"\"Of cows of many colours the milk has one colour; one sees knowledge as milk(-like), but (sees) the <em>liṅgins</em> as (variously coloured) cows.\"","para":"Cows differ in colour; their milk is one colour. Likewise, beings differ in form but <em>jñāna</em> (the Self) is one — <em>liṅgins</em> (embodied beings) appear varied like the cows.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>liṅgin</em> is the embodied <em>jīva</em> with its <em>liṅga-śarīra</em> / subtle body; embodiment is varied, knowledge is one."],["Sivananda","simple ethical reading — see the one <em>jñāna</em> across all <em>forms</em> of life."],["Krishnananda","the milk is <em>one in essence</em>; the cows are <em>many in upādhi</em>. Strong reading: <em>jñāna</em> is the <em>kṣīra</em>, not a product of the cows."],["Ayyangar","the verse is awkward; he supplies \"(but) the bodies as the various cows\" to clarify the elliptical syntax."],["Deussen","compares Śvetāśvatara 4.5 (<em>ajām ekāṃ lohita-śukla-kṛṣṇām</em>)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 20","title":"Churning the mind for the ghee of vijñāna","sa":["घृतमिव पयसि निगूढं भूते भूते च वसति विज्ञानम् ।","सततं मनसि मन्थयितव्यं मनो मन्थानभूतेन ॥ २० ॥"],"iast":["ghṛtam iva payasi nigūḍhaṃ bhūte bhūte ca vasati vijñānam |","satataṃ manasi manthayitavyaṃ mano manthāna-bhūtena || 20 ||"],"words":[["ghṛtam","n. nom. sg.","clarified butter"],["iva","indecl.","as"],["payasi","n. loc. sg. of payas","in milk"],["ni-gūḍham","n. nom. sg. pp.","hidden"],["bhūte bhūte","n. loc. sg. distrib.","in each being"],["ca vasati","conj. + 3 sg. pres.","and dwells"],["vijñānam","n. nom. sg.","direct knowledge"],["satatam","adv.","constantly"],["manasi","n. loc. sg.","in the mind / by means of the mind"],["manthayitavyam","n. nom. sg. gerundive of caus. math","to-be-churned"],["manaḥ","n. nom. sg.","the mind (itself)"],["manthāna-bhūtena","m. instr. sg.","(with the mind) become-the-churning-rod"]],"lit":"\"As ghee (is) hidden in milk, <em>vijñāna</em> dwells in every being; (it is) constantly to be churned out in the mind, the mind itself having become the churning-rod.\"","para":"<em>Vijñāna</em> is hidden in beings as butter in milk. Churn it out continuously, <em>using the very mind</em> as the churning rod.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","a striking image — the same <em>manas</em> that produced <em>saṃsāra</em> (v. 2) becomes the <em>mathana</em> by which <em>vijñāna</em> is extracted. This <em>reflexive</em> use of mind is the whole <em>sādhanā</em>."],["Sivananda","this is <em>manana-mathana</em> — sustained reflective churning, distinguished from desultory contemplation."],["Krishnananda","the simile is precise — the butter is <em>already there</em> in milk; <em>vijñāna</em> is not produced, only made manifest."],["Ayyangar","notes that this verse breaks the <em>anuṣṭubh</em> metre and is in a longer <em>vaitālīya/triṣṭubh</em>-like form — likely an interpolation, but textually firm."],["Deussen","identifies metric anomaly; compares Śvetāśvatara 1.15–16 (the fire-in-firesticks and ghee-in-milk pair)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 21","title":"Lifting the supreme as fire from the firesticks","sa":["ज्ञाननेत्रं समाधाय चोद्धरेद्वह्निवत्परम् ।","निष्कलं निश्चलं शान्तं तद्ब्रह्माहमिति स्मृतम् ॥ २१ ॥"],"iast":["jñāna-netraṃ samādhāya coddhared vahnivat param |","niṣkalaṃ niścalaṃ śāntaṃ tad brahmāham iti smṛtam || 21 ||"],"words":[["jñāna-netram","n. acc. sg. (kc.)","knowledge-as-the-eye / knowledge-as-leader"],["sam-ādhāya","ger. caus. of sam-ā-dhā","having well-established"],["ca","conj.","and"],["ud-dharet","3 sg. pot. of ud-hṛ","one should lift up"],["vahni-vat","adv.","fire-like (as the fire from the araṇi)"],["param","n. acc. sg.","the supreme"],["niṣ-kalam","n. nom. sg.","partless"],["niś-calam","n. nom. sg.","motionless"],["śāntam","n. nom. sg.","quiescent"],["tat brahma aham iti","quote-formula","\"that Brahman (am) I\""],["smṛtam","n. nom. sg. pp.","(is so) declared"]],"lit":"\"Having well-established knowledge as the (guiding) eye, one should lift up the supreme as (one lifts) fire (from the firesticks); 'partless, motionless, quiescent — that Brahman am I' — so it is declared.\"","para":"With <em>jñāna</em> itself as the instrument of vision, draw forth Brahman the way fire is drawn from the <em>araṇi</em>. \"I am that partless, motionless, peaceful Brahman\" — this is the formula.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","unites the <em>araṇi</em>-mathana of the Vedic ritual world with the <em>manas-mathana</em> of v. 20 — the same act, internalised."],["Sivananda","his standard <em>japa-mantra</em> drawn from this verse: \"<em>niṣkalaṃ niścalaṃ śāntaṃ tad-brahmāham iti smṛtam</em>\"."],["Krishnananda","notes the verse's three <em>lakṣaṇas</em> (partless / motionless / peaceful) parallel three Advaita <em>sat-cit-ānanda</em> aspects, but inverted as via-negativa."],["Ayyangar","\"...one should raise (the fire-like) supreme by means of the wood (of jñāna)...\" — gives the <em>fire-from-firesticks</em> reading explicitly."],["Deussen","compares Śvetāśvatara 1.14 (<em>svadehaṃ araṇiṃ kṛtvā, praṇavaṃ ca uttara-araṇim</em>)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 22","title":"I am Vāsudeva, indweller and grace-bestower","sa":["सर्वभूताधिवासं यद्भूतेषु च वसत्यपि ।","सर्वानुग्राहकत्वेन तदस्म्यहं वासुदेवः ॥ २२ ॥"],"iast":["sarva-bhūtādhivāsaṃ yad bhūteṣu ca vasaty api |","sarvānugrāhakatvena tad asmy ahaṃ vāsudevaḥ || 22 ||"],"words":[["sarva-bhūta-adhi-vāsam","n. acc. sg. (kc.)","the in-dweller-of-all-beings"],["yat","rel. pron. n. nom. sg.","which"],["bhūteṣu","n. loc. pl.","in (all) beings"],["ca vasati","conj. + 3 sg. pres.","and dwells"],["api","indecl.","also"],["sarva-anugrāhakatvena","n. instr. sg. abstr.","by way of being grace-giver to all"],["tat","pron. n. nom. sg.","that"],["asmi aham","1 sg. pres. + pron.","I am"],["vāsudevaḥ","m. nom. sg. (etymological pun on vas)","(the) Vāsudeva"]],"lit":"\"That which (is) the indweller-of-all-beings, and dwells in (all) beings, by being the grace-giver to all — that I am, (the) Vāsudeva.\"","para":"Brahman, indwelling in every being and giving grace to all — that I myself am: <em>Vāsudeva</em>. The closing identifies the supreme Self of the text with Vāsudeva-Kṛṣṇa by playing on the root <em>√vas</em> (\"to dwell\").","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","reads <em>vāsudeva</em> etymologically — \"He in whom all dwell, and who dwells in all\"; the closing identity is an Advaitic <em>mahā-vākya</em>, not a sectarian Vaiṣṇava intrusion."],["Sivananda","devotional reading — the Upaniṣad ends in <em>bhakti-jñāna-samuccaya</em>; <em>anugrāhakatva</em> is the <em>grace</em> dimension absent from v. 10's bare paramārtha."],["Krishnananda","notes the etymological play (<em>vāsu-deva = sarva-bhūteṣu vasati</em>) and the harmonising of Vedānta with Bhāgavata."],["Ayyangar","\"I am that Vāsudeva, who, by virtue of being the bestower of grace on all...\""],["Deussen","the only verse he explicitly marks as <em>Vaiṣṇava interpolation</em>; sees here a layer added when the Upaniṣad was assimilated into the Bhāgavata stream."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Closing peace-mantra","title":"","sa":["ॐ सह नाववतु... ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥ हरिः ॐ ॥","इति कृष्णयजुर्वेदेऽमृतबिन्दूपनिषत्समाप्ता ॥"],"iast":["iti kṛṣṇa-yajurvede 'mṛta-bindūpaniṣat samāptā ||"],"words":[],"lit":"Thus ends the Amṛtabindu Upaniṣad of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Thematic outline (Ayyangar's sectioning)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Verses</td><td>Theme</td><td>Key idea</td></tr><tr><td>1–3</td><td>Two minds</td><td>Mind as cause of bondage and liberation; make the mind <em>nirviṣaya</em>.</td></tr><tr><td>4–6</td><td>Withdrawn mind reaches Brahman</td><td><em>Nirodha</em> until heart-traffic ceases; Brahman is beyond thought-categories.</td></tr><tr><td>7–9</td><td><em>Oṃ</em> method and the unconditioned</td><td>Use <em>svara</em> to reach <em>asvara</em>; <em>niṣkala, nirvikalpa, nirañjana</em>.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>The <em>paramārtha</em> view</td><td>No bondage, no liberation.</td></tr><tr><td>11–14</td><td>One Self, many appearances</td><td><em>Avasthā-traya</em>, jīva = pot-space, pots break but space is untouched.</td></tr><tr><td>15–18</td><td>Word-māyā and the two vidyās</td><td>Break the <em>śabda-māyā</em>; master texts then discard them.</td></tr><tr><td>19–21</td><td>Similes for the one in many</td><td>Cow/milk, ghee/milk, fire/firesticks — same Self in all.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Closing identification</td><td>\"I am Vāsudeva.\"</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Reading recommendations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>Primary text</strong>: Sanskritdocuments.org `amrtabindu_upan.itx` (Devanāgarī + ITRANS).","- <strong>Classical Sanskrit commentary</strong>: Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin's <em>bhāṣya</em> — printed in <em>The Yoga Upaniṣads</em>, ed. A. Mahadeva Sastri, Adyar Library, 1920, with the Sanskrit commentary.","- <strong>English translation + commentary</strong>: T.R.S. Ayyangar, <em>The Yoga Upaniṣads</em>, Adyar Library, 1938 (reprint Adyar 1952). Single most useful one-volume reference for this text.","- <strong>Practical / meditative</strong>: Swami Sivananda, <em>Twenty Principal Upanishads</em> and <em>Mind: Its Mysteries and Control</em> (Divine Life Society) — uses Amṛtabindu v. 2 as thesis.","- <strong>Philosophical</strong>: Swami Krishnananda, audio lecture series on the minor Upaniṣads (Divine Life Society archive).","- <strong>Indological / textual</strong>: Paul Deussen, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (1897); English translation by V.M. Bedekar &amp; G.B. Palsule, <em>Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda</em>, Motilal Banarsidass, 1980, vol. 2 — for the 38-verse reconstruction."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Notes on this document","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- All Sanskrit follows the 22-verse Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda recension (Ayyangar / sanskritdocuments.org). Where MSS variants are known they are flagged in-line.","- IAST transliteration is given for every verse; word-by-word gloss includes grammatical notation (gender, case, number, finite-verb person/tense).","- Commentary summaries are <em>digests</em> in this compiler's words, drawing on the cited published works; they are not direct translations of the commentators. For citation-grade text, consult the primary editions listed above."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
